// mp-rs-est -- a plain store by a THIRD thread breaks the release sequence.
//
// rmm-zoo: litmus/cpp_memory_model/rs/ -- the release-sequence family, vendored
//   from gonzalobg/cpp_memory_model and checked in the zoo against the
//   per-version C++ cat models (cpp11.cat / cpp17.cat / cpp2w.cat, herd7 7.58).
// Source: ISO/IEC 14882:2011, :2017, :2020 (the [atomics.order] release-sequence
//   definition); Boehm, Giroux & Vafeiadis, P0668R5 "Revising the C++ Memory
//   Model" (2018); Boehm, P0982R1 "Weaken Release Sequences" (2018).
//
// Shape: a release store publishes data, a later store to the same flag does or
// does not continue the release sequence, and an acquiring reader does or does
// not synchronise.
//
// Reference verdicts (herd7 7.58, from the zoo's rs/README.md):
//   C++11 ALLOWS (racy)  |  C++17 ALLOWS (racy)  |  C++20 ALLOWS (racy)
//
// A relaxed store from a different thread was never part of the release
// sequence in any version, so reading 2 carries no release and the non-atomic
// read of the data races. This is the counterpart of mp-rs-eadd.lit: same
// third thread, a plain store instead of an RMW, opposite verdict.
//
// Upstream condition: exists([x]=2 /\ 1:a=2 /\ 1:b=0). The [x]=2 conjunct pins
// the coherence order (P0's release of 1 before P2's relaxed 2). MoRDor admits
// x = 2 and admits r1 = 2 && r2 = 0, but not their conjunction, so the
// assertion below is the projection onto the reader's registers; the coherence
// clause is recorded here rather than asserted. See ../../README.md.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  y.store(1, na);
  x.store(1, rel)
} ||| {
  r1 := x.load(acq);
  if (r1 = 2) {
    r2 := y.load(na)
  } else {skip}
} ||| {
  x.store(2, rlx)
}
%%
allow (r1 = 2 && r2 = 0) [C11]
